# ===========================================================================
# Build context for the production image.
#
# The image is assembled from exactly one file: the executable JAR Maven
# produced. Nothing else in this repository is read by the Dockerfile, so
# nothing else is sent. That is a large difference rather than a tidy one —
# `target/` alone holds compiled classes, the unpacked dependency set, test
# reports and the `.jar.original` sidecar, and the repository root holds `.git`.
# Excluding them keeps the context at a few tens of megabytes instead of
# hundreds, and — the part that matters — makes it impossible for a source file,
# a test, a migration script or a local `.env` to reach the runtime image by
# accident, because a file that is not in the context cannot be copied from it.
#
# The patterns are two levels because of a rule that catches people out: a file
# cannot be re-admitted by a negation if the directory that contains it was
# excluded. So the top level is excluded and the one directory that matters is
# re-admitted first, then the same is done one level down.
#
# Verify what is actually sent:
#
#   docker build --no-cache -t online-assignment-portal:local .   # prints the context size
#   docker run --rm --entrypoint sh online-assignment-portal:local -c 'ls -la /app'
# ===========================================================================

# Everything at the top level …
/*
# … except the build definition itself, which some builders refuse to locate in
# an ignored context …
!/Dockerfile
# … and the build output, which is re-admitted here only to be filtered below.
!/target

# Within the build output, everything …
/target/*
# … except the executable JAR — the whole reason this file exists. The name is
# spelled out to match the Dockerfile's COPY: a build that has not run, or a
# version bump in pom.xml, should fail loudly at the COPY rather than quietly
# package an empty context.
!/target/online-assignment-portal-0.0.1-SNAPSHOT.jar
